{:schema 1
 :date "2026-08-13"
 :receipt/type :ic02-diagnosis
 :method
 "Clause-prefix decomposition: run `dl/q` on the first clause, then the first
  two, then all three, and read the deltas. Non-invasive -- nothing inside the
  engine is modified, so the numbers are the engine's own. 8 iterations after 3
  warmups, one JVM, host load average 5.3 recorded by the probe itself."

 :measured-ms
 {:c1 {:clause [:?person "knows" :?friend] :ms 5.54 :rows 920 :note "?person bound by :in"}
  :c1+c2 {:added [:?msg "hasCreator" :?friend] :ms 32.06 :rows 995 :delta 26.52}
  :c1+c2+c3 {:added [:?msg "message/creationDate" :?date] :ms 45.65 :rows 995 :delta 13.59}
  :c2-alone {:ms 26.13 :rows 7355 :note "unbound: the whole hasCreator relation"}
  :c3-alone {:ms 22.74 :rows 7355 :note "unbound: the whole creationDate relation"}
  :c2-before-c1 {:ms 264.20 :rows 995 :note "same three clauses, bad order"}}

 :findings
 ["Adding `hasCreator` to 920 bindings costs 26.5 ms. Reading the ENTIRE
   hasCreator relation unbound costs 26.1 ms. They are the same number, so the
   step is paying for a full relation read -- which is the hash-join path, and is
   the correct choice at 7,355 rows against 920 groups."
  "The cost is therefore PER ROW, not per join step: 7,355 rows in 26.1 ms is
   3.5 microseconds a row, and 7,355 rows in 22.7 ms for creationDate is the same
   rate. IC02 touches roughly 15,000 quads across its two message clauses and
   spends about 45 ms doing it."
  "For scale, `estimate-cardinality` reads 286,744 rows out of the same indexes in
   0.0129 ms. The difference between reading an index and materialising rows from
   it is about four orders of magnitude, and execution is on the materialising
   side."]

 :refines-an-earlier-claim
 "2026-08-13-ic02-join-is-the-cost.edn said 'clause order is not the lever',
  from planned order 95.573 ms against source order 97.317 ms. That comparison
  was between two orders that happen to be nearly identical. Putting `hasCreator`
  before `knows` costs 264.20 ms against 45.65 ms -- 5.8x. Order matters
  enormously; what is true is that the planner already picks a good one, so
  there is no headroom ABOVE it. The earlier sentence was right about the
  conclusion and wrong about the reason."

 :next
 "The per-row cost inside the scan path, not the join algorithm. `query-seq`
  builds an {:s :p :o} map per candidate and `visible?` is applied to each; the
  join then hashes them into a set. Whether that allocation is the 3.5
  microseconds is the next thing to measure -- inside the engine this time, and
  before anything is changed."

 :caveats
 ["Absolute milliseconds move with host load: the same three-clause query measured
   95.6 ms at load ~8 earlier the same day and 45.7 ms at load 5.3 here. Compare
   within a run, not across."
  "One start person (the highest-degree one), one dataset, one JVM. A diagnosis."]}
